1
00:00:00,190 --> 00:00:03,040
Every song you’ve ever heard through headphones

2
00:00:02,790 --> 00:00:04,257
came from a tiny disc,

3
00:00:04,007 --> 00:00:06,193
shaking back and forth up to 20,000

4
00:00:05,943 --> 00:00:07,023
times a second.

5
00:00:07,523 --> 00:00:10,573
This is an ordinary pair of over-ear headphones.

6
00:00:10,323 --> 00:00:12,563
Soft cushions, a band, two cups.

7
00:00:12,313 --> 00:00:14,329
But peel back the ear cushion,

8
00:00:14,079 --> 00:00:16,271
and inside each cup sits a small,

9
00:00:16,021 --> 00:00:18,037
round machine called a driver.

10
00:00:19,707 --> 00:00:21,153
Lift its layers apart,

11
00:00:20,903 --> 00:00:22,837
and the driver becomes a stack.

12
00:00:22,587 --> 00:00:24,121
A thin diaphragm on top,

13
00:00:23,871 --> 00:00:25,512
the voice coil beneath it,

14
00:00:25,262 --> 00:00:26,368
then the magnet,

15
00:00:26,118 --> 00:00:27,437
all held in a frame.

16
00:00:27,187 --> 00:00:28,916
In many over-ear headphones,

17
00:00:28,666 --> 00:00:31,557
the whole thing is about forty millimetres across.

18
00:00:31,307 --> 00:00:32,416
Around its edge,

19
00:00:32,166 --> 00:00:34,776
a soft, flexible surround lets the diaphragm

20
00:00:34,526 --> 00:00:37,351
move freely, while keeping it perfectly centred.

21
00:00:38,341 --> 00:00:40,301
Start at the bottom, with the magnet.

22
00:00:40,051 --> 00:00:41,604
It’s often neodymium,

23
00:00:41,354 --> 00:00:45,201
a rare-earth magnet that’s remarkably strong for its size.

24
00:00:44,951 --> 00:00:48,094
Its field is focused into a narrow, ring-shaped gap.

25
00:00:48,654 --> 00:00:50,875
Into that gap drops the voice coil,

26
00:00:50,625 --> 00:00:52,733
a tiny spool of fine copper wire,

27
00:00:52,483 --> 00:00:54,534
attached to the diaphragm above.

28
00:00:54,284 --> 00:00:56,531
Your music arrives as a tiny electric

29
00:00:56,281 --> 00:00:59,014
current, changing thousands of times a second.

30
00:00:58,764 --> 00:01:01,122
When that signal flows through the coil,

31
00:01:00,872 --> 00:01:02,597
it becomes an electromagnet.

32
00:01:02,947 --> 00:01:04,733
Now the coil and the magnet push

33
00:01:04,483 --> 00:01:06,077
and pull against each other.

34
00:01:05,827 --> 00:01:06,948
Current one way,

35
00:01:06,698 --> 00:01:08,527
and the coil springs forward.

36
00:01:08,277 --> 00:01:10,697
Reverse the current, and it’s pulled back.

37
00:01:10,447 --> 00:01:12,393
Music is an alternating signal,

38
00:01:12,143 --> 00:01:14,253
so the coil shakes back and forth,

39
00:01:14,003 --> 00:01:15,949
exactly in time with the sound.

40
00:01:16,669 --> 00:01:19,145
And because the coil is fixed to the diaphragm,

41
00:01:18,895 --> 00:01:20,281
the diaphragm moves too,

42
00:01:20,031 --> 00:01:21,749
pushing and pulling on the air.

43
00:01:21,499 --> 00:01:24,301
Those tiny pressure waves travel into your ear,

44
00:01:24,051 --> 00:01:25,767
and you hear them as music.

45
00:01:26,117 --> 00:01:28,314
A deep bass note moves it slowly,

46
00:01:28,064 --> 00:01:29,967
as few as 20 times a second.

47
00:01:29,717 --> 00:01:31,938
The highest notes we can hear move

48
00:01:31,688 --> 00:01:33,387
it 20,000 times a second.

49
00:01:33,137 --> 00:01:34,918
So a good diaphragm has to be

50
00:01:34,668 --> 00:01:35,763
light and stiff,

51
00:01:35,513 --> 00:01:37,876
to keep up without bending out of shape.

52
00:01:38,726 --> 00:01:40,276
And there are two drivers,

53
00:01:40,026 --> 00:01:41,076
one in each cup,

54
00:01:40,826 --> 00:01:42,976
each fed its own channel of the music.

55
00:01:42,726 --> 00:01:44,720
That’s what lets a guitar sound like

56
00:01:44,470 --> 00:01:45,592
it’s on your left,

57
00:01:45,342 --> 00:01:47,336
and a voice sit right in the middle.

58
00:01:47,686 --> 00:01:49,146
Here’s something lovely.

59
00:01:48,896 --> 00:01:50,734
Run the whole thing backwards,

60
00:01:50,484 --> 00:01:52,216
and it becomes a microphone.

61
00:01:51,966 --> 00:01:53,574
Sound moves the diaphragm,

62
00:01:53,324 --> 00:01:55,767
the coil moves through the magnet’s field,

63
00:01:55,517 --> 00:01:57,281
and a tiny current flows out.

64
00:01:58,022 --> 00:01:59,612
Put it all back together.

65
00:01:59,362 --> 00:02:00,515
The signal flows,

66
00:02:00,265 --> 00:02:01,311
the coil pulls,

67
00:02:01,061 --> 00:02:02,427
the diaphragm pushes,

68
00:02:02,177 --> 00:02:04,392
and the air carries the music to you.

69
00:02:04,142 --> 00:02:06,560
All inside something that fits in the palm

70
00:02:06,310 --> 00:02:07,232
of your hand.

71
00:02:06,982 --> 00:02:08,030
The same idea,

72
00:02:07,780 --> 00:02:10,424
scaled up, drives most of the loudspeakers

73
00:02:10,174 --> 00:02:11,165
in the world.

74
00:02:11,725 --> 00:02:14,505
A disc that shakes 20,000 times a second.

75
00:02:14,255 --> 00:02:15,865
Now picture the opposite:

76
00:02:15,615 --> 00:02:17,796
a tiny wheel that swings just eight

77
00:02:17,546 --> 00:02:18,623
times a second,

78
00:02:18,373 --> 00:02:20,995
and keeps time to within two seconds a day.

79
00:02:20,745 --> 00:02:23,757
That’s inside a Rolex, linked right below.

